    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-20869",
      "knowledge_point_id": "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit",
      "problem": "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we substitute $u=2+{x}^{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2+{x}^{4}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\infty }^{2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{(2+{x}^{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{u}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
